WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
You're buying a product online,
and you see 3 different sellers.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.260
They're all offering that same product,
at essentially the same price.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.860
One of them has a 100% positive rating,
but with only 10 reviews.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.980
Another has a 96 positive rating with 50 total reviews.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.600
And yet another has a 93% positive rating,
but with 200 total reviews.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.440
Which one should you buy from?

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:28.100
[music]

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.100
I think we all have this instinct that
the more data we see,

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.420
it gives us more confidence in a given rating.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.080
We're a little suspicious of seeing 100% ratings

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.320
since, more often than not,
they come from a tiny number of reviews.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.520
Which makes it feel more plausible that things could
have gone another way, and given a lower rating.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.240
But how do you make that intuition quantitative?

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.980
What's the rational way to reason
about the trade-off here,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.480
between the confidence gained from more data,
versus the lower absolute percentage?

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.100
This particular example, is a slight modification from
one than John Cook gave in his excellent blog post

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.300
"A Bayesian view of Amazon Resellers".

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.960
For you and me, it's a wonderful excuse to dig into
a few core topics in probability and statistics.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.220
And to really cover these topics properly, it takes time,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.180
so what I'm gonna do is break this down into three videos.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.380
Where, in this first one,
we'll set up a model for the situation

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.000
and start by talking about the binomial distribution.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.400
The second is gonna bring in ideas of
Bayesian updating

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.440
and how to work with probabilities
over continuous values.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.440
And in the third, we'll look at something known as a
Beta distribution

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.300
and pull up a little Python to analyze the data we have,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.220
and come to various different answers
depending on what it is you want to optimize.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.040
Now, to throw you a bone
before we dive into all the math,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.720
let me just show you
what one of the answers turns out to be,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.460
since it's delightfully simple.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.720
When you see an online rating,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.720
something like this 10/10,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.080
you pretend that there were two more reviews.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.500
One that was positive and one that's negative.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.960
In this case, that means you pretend that it's 11/12,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.280
which would give 91.7%.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.400
This number is your probability of
having a good experience with that seller.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.840
So in the case of 50 reviews,
where you have 48 positive and 2 negative,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.800
you pretend that it's really 49 positive and 3 negative,

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:20.020
which would give you 49/52, or 94.2%.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.200
That's your probability of having a good experience with the second seller.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.940
Playing the same game with
our third seller who had 200 reviews,

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:34.080
you get 187/202 or 92.6%.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.800
So, according this rule, it would mean your best bet is
to go with seller number two.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.680
This is something known as Laplace's rule of succession
- it dates back to the 18th century -

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.780
and to understand what assumptions
are underlying this,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.080
and how changing either those assumptions or
your underlying goal can change the choice you make,

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.220
we really do need to go through all the math.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.380
So without further ado, let's dive in!

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.540
[Music]

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:06.380
Step 1: How exactly are we modeling the situation
and what exactly is it that you want to optimize?

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.660
One option is to think of each seller
as producing random experiences,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.460
that are either positive or negative,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.700
and that each seller has some kind of constant
underlying probability of giving a good experience,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.520
what we're gonna call the "success rate," or "s" for short.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.260
The whole challenge is that we don't know "s."

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.660
When we see that first rating of 100% with 10 reviews, that doesn't mean the underlying success rate is 100%

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.460
It could very well be something like 95%.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.460
And just to illustrate, let me run a little simulation

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.140
where I choose a random number between 0 and 1,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.400
and if it's less than 0.95, I'll record it as a positive review

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.440
otherwise, I'll record it as a negative review.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.960
Now do this ten times in a row...

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.400
And then make ten more...

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.360
And ten more...

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.900
And ten more, and so on

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.980
to get a sense of, what sequences of 10 reviews,

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:04.900
for a seller with this success rate, 0.95,
would tend to look like.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.640
Quite a few of those - around 60% actually - give 10/10.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:15.180
So the data we see seems perfectly plausible if the seller's true success rate was 95%.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.680
Or... maybe it's really 90%, or 99%.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.920
The whole challenge is that we just don't know.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.340
As to the goal, let's say you simply want to maximize your probability of having a positive experience,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.160
despite not being sure of the success rate.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.360
So, think about this here.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.420
We've got many different possible success rates
for each seller,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.700
any number from zero up to one,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.040
and we need to say something about how likely
each one of these success rates is,

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:44.100
a kind of probability of probabilities.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.640
Unlike the more gamified examples
like coin flips and die tosses and

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.100
the sort of things you see in an intro probability class,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.700
where you go in assuming a long-run frequency,
like 1/2 or 1/6,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.960
what we have here is uncertainty about
the long-run frequency itself,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.120
which is a much more potent kind of doubt.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.220
I should also emphasize that this kind of setup

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.200
is relevant to many, many situations in the real world,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.780
where you need to make a judgement
about a random process from limited data.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:16.040
For example, let's say that you're
setting up a factory producing cars,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.060
and in an initial test of 100 cars,
2 of them come out with some kind of problem.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.760
If you plan to spin things up to produce a million cars,

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.180
what are you willing to *confidently* say

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.680
about how many total cars
will have problems that need addressing?

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.740
It's not like the test *guarantees*
that the true error rate is 2%,

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.780
but what exactly *does* it say?

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.740
As your first challenge, let me ask you this:

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.720
if you did magically know
the true success rate for a given seller, say it was 95%,

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.980
how would you compute
the probability of seeing,

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:52.460
say 10 positive reviews and 0 negative reviews?

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:56.400
Or 48 and 2? Or 186 and 14?

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:02.200
In other words, what's the probability of seeing the data given an assumed success rate?

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.920
A moment ago, I showed you something like this
with a simulation, generating 10 random reviews.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.180
And, with a little programming, you could just
do that many times,

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.680
building up a histogram to get some sense of
what this distribution looks like.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:21.360
[music]

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:26.900
Likewise, you could simulate sets of 50 reviews
and get some sense for how probable it would be

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.340
to see 48 positive and 2 negative.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.720
You see, this is the nice thing about probability;

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:37.140
a little programming can almost always let you "cheat"
a little and see what the answer is ahead of time by simulating it

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:41.100
For example, after a few hundred thousand samples
of 50 reviews,

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.500
assuming the success rate is 95%,

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:48.360
it looks like about 26.1% of them
would give us this 48/50 review.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.520
Luckily, in this case, an exact formula is not bad at all.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.880
The probability of seeing exactly 48/50 looks like this.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.920
This first term is pronounced "50 choose 48",

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.160
and it represents the total number of ways
that you could take 50 slots

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.000
and fill out 48 of them.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.440
For example, maybe you start with 48 good reviews
and end with 2 bad reviews.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:17.400
Or maybe you start with 47 good reviews,
and then it goes bad-good-bad

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:18.740
and so on.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:24.720
In principle, if you were to enumerate every single
possible way of filling 48/50 slots like this,

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:32.060
the total number of these patterns is "50 choose 48", which, in this case, works out to be 1225.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:37.060
What do we multiply by this count?
Well it's the probability of any one of these patterns.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:41.320
Which is the probability of a single positive review,
raised to the 48th [power],

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.980
times the probability of a single negative review squared.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:49.080
Crucial is that we assume each review
is independant of the last,

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.200
so we can multiply all the probabilities together like this,

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:57.360
and with the numbers we have, when you evaluate it,
it works out to be 0.261,

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.320
which matches what we just saw empirically
with the simulation.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.860
You could also replace this 48
with some other value

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.920
and compute the probability of seeing
any other number of positive reviews.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.340
Again, assuming a given success rate.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.960
What you're looking at right now,
by the way,

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.920
is known in the business as a binomial distribution,

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.360
one of THE most fundamental distributions
in probability.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:26.500
It comes up whenever you have something like
a coin flip,

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:30.860
a random event that can go one of two ways,
and you repeat it some number of times.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:35.360
And what you want to know is the probability
of getting various different totals.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:43.100
For our purposes, this formula gives us the probability of
seeing the data, given an assumed success rate,

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:47.240
Which, ultimately, we want to somehow use
to make judgments about the opposite:

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.020
the probability of a success rate
given the fixed data that we see.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.520
These are related, but definitely distinct.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.020
To get more in that direction,
let's play around with this value of "s"

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.400
and see what happens as we change it
to different numbers between 0 and 1.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.960
The binomial distribution that it produces

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.880
kind of looks like this pile
that's centered around whatever "s" times 50 is.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:15.660
The value we care about,
the probability of seeing 48 out of 50 reviews,

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.060
is represented by this highlighted 48th bar.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.840
So let's draw a second plot on the bottom
representing how that value depends on "s".

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:30.440
When "s" is equal to 0.96,
that value is as high as it's ever gonna get.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:32.360
And this should kind of makes sense.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.340
Because when you look at that review of 96%,

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.080
it should be most likely
if the true underlying success rate was 96%.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:46.240
As "s" increases, it kind of peters out;
going to 0 as "s" approaches 1.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.780
Since someone with a perfect success rate
would never have those 2 negative reviews.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.680
Also, as you move to the left,
it approaches 0 pretty quickly.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.680
By the time you get to "s" = 0.8,

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.360
getting 48 out of 50 reviews by chance
is exceedingly rare.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.280
It would happen 1 in a 1000 times.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.400
This plot we have on the bottom is a great start
to getting a more quantitative description,

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.560
for which *values* of "s" feel more or less plausible.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:19.960
Written down as a formula, what I want you to remember
is that, as a function of the success rate s,

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:22.060
the curve looks like some constant

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.780
times "s" to the number of positive reviews

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:28.240
times 1 minus "s" to the number of negative reviews.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:34.460
In principle, if we had more data,
like 480 positive reviews and 20 negative reviews,

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:39.900
the resulting plot would still be centered around .96,
but it would be smaller and more concentrated.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:44.380
A good exercise right now would be to see
if you could explain *why* that's the case.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.580
There is a lingering question, though,
of what to actually *do* with these curves.

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:54.720
I mean, our goal is to compute the probability
that you have a good experience with this seller.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:56.040
So what do you do?

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:02.540
Naively, you might think that probability is 96%
since that's where the peak of the graph is,

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.160
which, in a sense, is the most likely success rate.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:08.260
But think of the example with 10 out of 10 positives.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:13.560
In that case, the whole binomial formula simplifies to be "s" to the power 10.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:17.720
The probability of seeing 10 consecutive good reviews

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.840
is the probability of seeing *one* of them
raised to the 10th.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:26.960
The closer the true success rate is to 1,
the higher the probability of seeing a 10 out of 10.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:31.200
Our plot on the bottom only ever increases
as "s" approaches 1.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:38.080
But even if "s = 1" is the value that maximizes this probability, surely you wouldn't feel comfortable saying

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.720
that, you personally, have a 100% probability
of a good experience with this seller.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.880
Maybe you think that, instead, we should look for
some kind of center of mass of this graph

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.520
and that would absolutely be
on the right track.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.880
First though, we need to explain
how to take *this* expression

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.480
for the probability of the data we're seeing,
given a value of "s",

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:02.720
and get the probability *for* a value of "s"
(the thing we actually don't know)

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.080
given the data (the thing we actually know).

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:10.300
And that requires us to talk about Bayes rule
and also probability density functions.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.660
For those, I'll see you in part two.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:33.040
[music]

